Q: How do I pass a server variable to client side JS in astro? A: <p>The data-attribute approach from the docs works fine for strings. Once the data is an object or array though, you end up JSON.stringifying it into the markup and parsing it back by hand, and that manual version is where security mistakes creep in.</p>
<p>Something like this looks harmless:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;script is:inline set:html={`window.data = ${JSON.stringify(data)};`} /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>But <code>JSON.stringify</code> does not escape <code>&lt;</code>. If any string value in your data contains <code>&lt;/script&gt;</code>, it closes the tag early and the browser parses the rest of the payload as HTML. If that data ever comes from user input, this is an XSS vector. <code>define:vars</code> has a different tradeoff: it turns your script into an inline, unbundled script, so you lose imports and TypeScript, and it complicates a strict Content-Security-Policy.</p>
<p>I maintain a small utility, <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@ayco/astro-resume" rel="nofollow noreferrer">@ayco/astro-resume</a>, that packages the safe version of this pattern. Zero runtime dependencies, works on Astro 4+:</p>
<pre><code>---
import Serialize from &quot;@ayco/astro-resume&quot;;

const data = {
    hello: &quot;world&quot;,
    isOkay: true,
};
export type Data = typeof data;
---

&lt;Serialize id=&quot;my-data&quot; data={data} /&gt;

&lt;script&gt;
import { deserialize } from &quot;@ayco/astro-resume&quot;;
import type { Data } from &quot;./ThisComponent.astro&quot;;

const data = deserialize&lt;Data&gt;(&quot;my-data&quot;);
console.log(data); // { hello: &quot;world&quot;, isOkay: true }
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Your client script stays a normal bundled module, so imports and TypeScript keep working.</p>
<p>Security practices worth following whichever route you take, all of which the package enforces or nudges you toward:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Escape on serialize.</strong> The serialized output escapes <code>&lt;</code>, so a <code>&lt;/script&gt;</code> inside your data cannot break out of the element. If you roll your own, you must do this yourself.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Treat deserialized data as untrusted.</strong> <code>deserialize&lt;T&gt;()</code> returns <code>unknown</code> by default, so you can't read properties off it until you name a type. For data you don't fully control, pass a custom parser (a Zod schema's <code>parse</code>, for example) as the second argument; it's typed <code>(serialized: string) =&gt; T</code>, so validation and the return type stay in sync.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Assume everything you serialize is public.</strong> It ends up in the HTML, visible in view-source. API keys, tokens, and other secrets should never go through this path; keep those server-side behind an endpoint.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Disclosure: I'm the author of the package. The pattern above (JSON in the document, parsed on the client) is the same one Astro itself uses internally for island props, so you can also manually write it; the package just handles the escaping and typing for you.</p>

Q: Rounded Swing JButton using Java A: <p>Since the borders were not filled correctly, I used <code>@override</code>:</p>
<pre><code>@Override
    public void paintBorder(Component c, Graphics g, int x, int y, int width, int height) {
        final Color bgColor = c.getParent().getBackground();
        final Color color = c.getBackground();
        g.setColor(bgColor);
        g.fillRect(0, 0, 1, 1);
        g.fillRect(width - 1, height - 1, 1, 1);
        g.fillRect(0, height - 1, 1, 1);
        g.fillRect(width - 1, 0, 1, 1);
        g.setColor(color);
        g.drawRoundRect(x, y, width - 1, height - 1, radius, radius);
    }
</code></pre>

Q: Initialization of all elements of a multidimensional std::array to one default value A: <p>Multidimensional contiguous array is a way more complicated concept than meets the eye that's why C++ is still struggling with standardizing a certain interface for it. The fixed sized <code>std::array</code> provides the luxury of <em>nested array</em> definition as illusrtated in the OP. In contrast, the dynamic array <code>std::vector</code>should not be jagged/nested as in OP. C++23 provides <code>std::md_span</code>, but prefer C++20 <code>&lt;ranges&gt;</code> for the task:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;ranges&gt;
auto constexpr d_min = 2z,
               d_mid = 5z,
               d_max = 2z;

std::vector vec // 20 × 2.0
(d_max * d_mid * d_max, 2.0d);

std::ranges::fill(vec, -1);

//as if: double (&amp;vec3)[d_max][d_mid][d_min] = vec;

//Requires std=c++23
auto vec3d
= vec                      //[max * mid * min]
| std::views::chunk(d_min) //[max * mid] [min]
| std::views::chunk(d_mid);//[max] [mid] [min]
</code></pre>
<p>This technique can work with <code>std::array</code> or C++26 <code>std::inplace_vector</code> as well. But <code>vec3d</code> will be a <em>view(≈reference)</em> to the original object, and should not be returned from creating scope of the original object - to avoid dangling.</p>
<p>With nesting approach however, <code>std::array</code> can benefit from <code>&lt;ranges&gt;</code> a different way too:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>using a_min = std::array&lt;double, d_min&gt;;
using a_mid = std::array&lt;a_min, d_mid&gt;;
using a_max = std::array&lt;a_mid, d_max&gt;;

a_max arr3d {};

auto arr_flat
= arr3d      v v v //[max] [mid] [min]
| std::views::join //[max * mid] [min]
| std::views::join;//[max * mid * min]

std::ranges::fill(arr_flat, -1);
</code></pre>
<p>Again <code>arr_flat</code> is <em>view</em>, and shouldn't be returned from creation scope of <code>arr</code>. It would be best if I had sent the result of second <code>join</code> directly to <code>fill</code>, but I introduced the temporary view <code>arr_flat</code> for illustration purpose.</p>
<p>I hope you can upgrade to <code>std=c++23</code>, because it makes code a lot easier to maintain, and read.</p>
